## 00896 PATIENT=1 DOCTOR=2
1: Doctor, I've got that awful pain again in my right side, like the kidney stone I had before. ~ cc s1
2: I'm sorry to hear that. When did it start this time? ~ s1 u1
1: Yesterday evening, I think. Or maybe the night before, honestly I lost track. ~ s1 u1
2: Where do you feel it? ~ s1
1: Right side of my back, going round to the front. ~ s1
2: Is it constant, or does it come in waves? ~ s1
1: Waves. It builds, then eases off. ~ s1
2: Have you passed any blood in your urine? ~ n1
1: No blood that I've seen, but it's dark. ~ n1
2: Any fever or shivering? ~
1: No fever. ~
2: What medicines are you on at the moment? ~ m1 m2
1: Tamsulosin, 400 micrograms, once a day, from the last time. And diclofenac, 50 mg, three times a day, when the pain is bad. ~ m1 m2
2: Is the tamsulosin helping? ~
1: I think so. It's not as bad as last time. ~
2: Do you have any history of kidney stones, or other kidney problems? ~ h1
1: One stone, two years ago. It passed by itself. ~ h1
2: Let me check your numbers. Pulse, pressure, temperature. ~
1: Okay. ~ pause=10
2: Pulse 84, blood pressure 136 over 86, temperature 36.9, oxygen 98 percent, breathing 16. ~ v
1: All right? ~
2: Nothing alarming, given you're in pain. I'd like to send a urine sample and do a blood test for your kidney function. ~ p1 p2
1: Okay. Can I give the urine sample here? ~
2: The nurse will show you the toilet. ~
1: And what happens after? ~
2: Depending on the results, a scan might be needed, but I'd rather wait for the tests first. ~
1: Should I keep taking the diclofenac? ~
2: Yes, with food, and not for more than a few days. Drink plenty of water through the day. ~ c1
1: How much? ~
2: Aim for two to three litres. ~ c2
1: I'll try. ~
2: If you get a fever, vomiting, or the pain gets suddenly worse, come to the emergency department. ~ c3
1: Right. Thank you. ~
